WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:04.036 line:0%
-We will help you carve out
the most problematic areas

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:06.005 line:0%
and what we can do with them
because I think

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:08.340 line:0%
a common misconception
around death cleaning

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.578 line:0%
is that we're just here to like
throw things out and declutter

00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:13.246 line:0%
and like we're done with it.

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:15.882 line:0%
But that's not what
it's about. At its heart,

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:19.385 line:0%
it's about discovering
what's important to you.

00:00:19.385 --> 00:00:23.456 line:0%
-Yeah.
-And what are your hopes
for these things?

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:24.657
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:27.193
We're here with Amy Poehler,
everybody.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:32.265
That was a clip from "The Gentle
Art of Swedish Death Cleaning."

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:34.600
"The Gentle Art of Swedish
Death Cleaning."

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.436
Not a show when I met you
almost two decades ago

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:39.438
I would have thought
would be a show you'd produce.

00:00:39.438 --> 00:00:42.575
-Yeah, well, I'm always
growing and changing and...

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.043
But it's so cool.

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:48.581
It's based off of a book,
and it is this idea of kind of

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:51.317
kind of how do you want to go
through your things?

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:52.418
How do you want
to live your life?

00:00:52.418 --> 00:00:53.652
What conversations
do you want to have

00:00:53.652 --> 00:00:54.654
while you're still around

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:56.589
and what stuff
can you get rid of

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:59.959
to not burden the people
who are left when you're gone?

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:04.797
So I do the voice-over for it.
We produced it along with Scout,

00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:06.432
the great producers
who do "Queer Eye."

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:09.469
We have three great
Swedish death cleaners

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:12.538
who come in
and are like incredibly

00:01:12.538 --> 00:01:15.241
Swedish in the best --
in the best way.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:16.976
I mean that because
they're so direct.

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:19.745
-Yes.
-And I think clarity is
kindness.

00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:21.614
I like direct people.
-Yep.

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:23.416
-As you can probably tell.

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:27.487
And so they just say very --
They get to the point.

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:29.755
-They're almost the opposite
of magicians.

00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:32.859
-Uh, 100%.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:35.528
Magicians are like,
"I'm over here, I'm over there."

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:38.164
And you're like,
"No, you're going to jail.

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:41.233
You're in jail. That's where
you should be -- in jail."

00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:45.471
-So your brother, married
to a wonderful Swedish woman,

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:47.173
has a Swedish family,
lives in Sweden.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.843
So you've become very --
you've become very aware

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:52.145
of the beautiful benefits
of the Swedes.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:53.713
-And you would think
I could speak more Swedish,

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:55.682
but I can't.
But I love that country.

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:58.484
And my brother lives there
with his family and yeah,

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:00.552
and the Swedes have this way of
just saying like,

00:02:00.552 --> 00:02:01.988
"This is ugly."

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:03.923
[ Laughter ]

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.025
"You don't like it and we don't
like it

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:07.826
and you need to go --
it needs to go away."

00:02:07.826 --> 00:02:09.762
And you're just like, "Wow."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:12.699
They say it really fast
and I like that about them.

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:15.334
-And do you find having, like,
engaged with this,

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:17.670
do you find you're good
at that now in your life?

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:18.771
Are you getting rid of things?

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:20.873
Are there things
that you're keeping

00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:24.510
because you want to be able
to pass them on?

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.479
-Yeah, I think, right?
Everybody's trying to figure out

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:27.846
what they should keep
for their kids,

00:02:27.846 --> 00:02:29.916
what their kids will like,
especially we have kids,

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:33.452
and you think about what their
first apartment,

00:02:33.452 --> 00:02:35.888
if they would like
this old rotary phone.

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:38.324
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:41.794
You're like, "How many DVDs
are they going to be into?"

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:43.062
-They might come back.

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:44.530
I feel as though by the time
your kids go to college.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.199
-Well, not --

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:49.969
I do feel, though,
that A.I. is going to kill us.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:51.537
-Uh-huh.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:53.105
-And I, you know,
we're Gen X,

00:02:53.105 --> 00:02:55.875
so I think we are
the last generation

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:58.111
that are not going
to mate with robots.

00:02:58.111 --> 00:03:00.046
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:03.249
I think we will
fight against the robots.

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:06.085
But Gen Z will love
and marry robots.

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:07.386
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:09.622
And that will be hard
because we will be obviously

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:12.424
waging some sort of sort
of underground revolution

00:03:12.424 --> 00:03:14.293
against the robots
and at the same time,

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:15.728
your sons are going
to bring them home.

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:17.597
-Exactly, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:19.198
-And then you're
going to have to act

00:03:19.198 --> 00:03:20.667
like you like the robot.

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:22.434
-Exactly. You're going to
have to make the choice.

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:26.839
Like, do I save
my half robot, half human son?

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.108
-Yeah.
-Or do I kill him because I know

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.710
he's going to put
worms in my brain

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.178
or whatever they're going to do?

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.912
I can't even imagine
what -- I'm no robot.

00:03:33.912 --> 00:03:35.515
I'm a human.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:37.683
But I don't like it.
and I won't date a robot.

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:38.784
-You won't?
-No.

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:40.186
-Interesting. Even if --

00:03:40.186 --> 00:03:42.454
-Nope. No matter how hard
you try.

00:03:42.454 --> 00:03:44.123
-Can I try to sell you on it?
Alright.

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:45.892
-You're not going to sell me
on it. I'm not dating a robot.

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.327
-Okay, but here's the thing.

00:03:47.327 --> 00:03:50.596
Here's how the A.I. love robot
that we're developing works.

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:52.431
At first it's going
to be bad, right?

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:54.767
It's not going to work out.
But each successive date,

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.003
the A.I. will learn
more about you.

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:59.772
And then -- so by like date 10,
you'll be like,

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:02.508
"Oh, we really understand."
You have to feed the A.I. robot.

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:04.177
-Oh, I have to feed it?

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:05.345
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:08.214
Oh, great. So it's like
a human man, then?

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:09.382
Is that what you're
talking about?

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:12.918
[ Laughter ]
I have to tell it what I like

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:15.288
and then it knows?
-Yeah.

00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:17.757
Also, you got to plug it in. You
got to remember to plug it in.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.526
-I have to return every couple
of years --

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:22.060
I'm like, "It's broken.
I need a new --"

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.162
It's like the iPhone.

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:25.231
I'm like, "I need
a new robot boyfriend."

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:28.434
No, I won't date a robot
no matter how hard you try.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:30.903
Unless...
-Okay.

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:32.571
-He picks up food
on the way home.

00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:35.108
[ Laughter ]
-Wait. That's the one thing?

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:37.477
I feel like robots
almost already do that.

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:39.645
-Yeah. Oh, yeah.
Those robots that, like, go --

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:40.813
Have you seen them
on the street?

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:42.548
Well, you guys probably
don't care

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:44.150
because you're all Gen Z,
and you're like,

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.618
"That's cute."
-Yeah.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:48.721
-It's not going to be cute
when you go home

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:51.023
and you lift up your covers
and it's living there

00:04:51.023 --> 00:04:53.726
and it's going to kill you
and strangle you.

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:57.029
-Do you feel like --
Are you overwhelmed

00:04:57.029 --> 00:04:59.565
by the technology
your kids are engaging with?

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:02.100
-Yeah. Although, you know,
have you tried that chat --

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.404
Maybe you guys have done bits
on it here about ChatGPT.

00:05:05.404 --> 00:05:07.974
-Yeah, no, we haven't really
messed around with it much.

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:09.208
-You haven't messed
around with it?
-No.

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:11.944
-Okay. So it can't
really write a joke yet.

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:16.082
I don't think. And once it
writes a joke, like game's over.

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:17.650
-Yeah. No, that's actually --
-That's it.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.053
-We're just going to do
one real sad last show.

00:05:20.053 --> 00:05:21.854
-Yeah.

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:23.455
-There's going to be
a robot dressed

00:05:23.455 --> 00:05:25.057
just like me
sitting there waiting.

00:05:25.057 --> 00:05:26.859
-Yeah.
-Like [Whirring]

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:31.063
-And then it's just like, yeah,
it can't write comedy yet,

00:05:31.063 --> 00:05:33.166
but, I mean, I'm sure
it will in like a week.

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.301
-Yeah. This is like --
You know what?

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:37.236
It's so great that you're here
on our last week.

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:38.904
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.904 --> 00:05:41.474
-Our last human week.
-Oh, man.

00:05:41.474 --> 00:05:43.809
What a great thing to have you
here on our last human week.

00:05:43.809 --> 00:05:45.444
You were here
on our first human week,

00:05:45.444 --> 00:05:46.679
and you were here on our last.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:47.847
-I know you're --

00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:50.015
When is your anniversary
for your 10 years?

00:05:50.015 --> 00:05:51.350
Next year?
-Next February is 10 years.

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:52.618
-Wow. That's awesome.
-Isn't that crazy?

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:54.386
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:54.386 --> 00:05:55.655
And I always say it.

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:57.356
I mean, I got to give credit
where credit is due

00:05:57.356 --> 00:06:00.259
because our first show,
you were first.

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:02.995
It was very important to me
that you were our first guest.

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:05.664
And then that meant we
had to ask the second guest,

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:09.101
who was a young fellow named
Vice President Joe Biden.

00:06:09.101 --> 00:06:10.302
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.302 --> 00:06:11.670
Now, usually vice presidents,

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:13.906
when they're on a 12:30 show,
would go first.

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:15.541
-Yes.
-And we did say to Joe Biden,

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:17.076
"Would you be okay following
Amy Poehler?"

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:19.412
And to his credit, he was like,
"I'll follow her anywhere."

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:23.215
So very, very charming,
very sweet of him.

00:06:23.215 --> 00:06:24.684
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:26.184
Well, it's lovely
to have you here, as always.

00:06:26.184 --> 00:06:30.289
How about break a leg, wherever
you may bring your legs?

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.391
I'm very excited for
the Restless Leg tour.

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:33.725
I think we're all very excited.

00:06:33.725 --> 00:06:35.761
-Thank you very much, Seth.
I really appreciate it.

00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:37.229
And I love coming here.
You know I love you.

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:38.531
Thank you so much.
I love you. Goodbye.

00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:40.366
-Pick up the check.
Pick up the check.

00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:43.736
Pick up the check.
Amy Poehler, everybody.

